  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 149 AM MDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nWednesday will bring a fairly similar setup, but probably with \nbetter surface moisture due to models picking up on slightly more \norganized southerly flow across the High Plains. While it is still \nearly, parameters look somewhat favorable for strong to severe \nthunderstorms both Tuesday and Wednesday, despite lower overall \nthunderstorm coverage compared to the monsoon surge our area has \nbeen under for the last several days. The ECMWF ensemble Extreme \nForecast Index for combined CAPE-Shear is around 0.7 to 0.85 for \nmuch of the High Plains both Tuesday and Wednesday, indicating that \na large proportion of ECMWF ensemble members have combined CAPE-\nshear values that are climatologically unusual to extreme. We will \nneed to continue to watch this period in the coming days. \n\nA more defined upper level shortwave trough will pass through the \narea Wednesday evening. Behind this feature, the upper level ridge \nover the southwest will begin to re-amplify to our west, initiating \na drier, northwest flow pattern for the remainder of the week. \nPrecipitable water won't be terrible during this weather pattern, so \nwe should still see at least a few isolated PM showers and storms \nThursday and Friday, but expect those two days to be considerably \ndrier. Temperatures will also begin to climb once again. 700-mb \ntemperatures climb back to near the climatological 90th percentile \nby Friday into Saturday, peaking around +16 to +18C across the area. \nExpect high temperatures to climb above seasonal averages Thursday, \nand then closer to 5 to 10 degrees above average Friday and \nSaturday. Since we'll be somewhat on the periphery of this heat \nwave, current forecasts do not challenge daily record highs, but \nthis could still change in the coming days. Over the course of \nThursday through Saturday, the upper level ridge should start to \nvery slowly meander back to the east. Once this moves east of the \nFour Corners, the monsoon should surge back to the north, and we may \nsee precipitation chances increasing again by Saturday.",
